---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Common problems">
    <h1>Common problems</h1>

    <p class="docs-lead">
        Basic diagnostic steps for common Octlitch desktop, networking,
        package-management and hardware problems.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-note">
        <strong>Before troubleshooting</strong>

        <p>
            Install available system updates first unless an update itself
            appears to have caused the problem.
        </p>
    </div>

    <pre><code>sudo dnf upgrade --refresh</code></pre>

    <h2>Collect basic system information</h2>

    <p>
        When reporting a problem, it is useful to include information about
        the system, kernel and desktop environment.
    </p>

    <pre><code>cat /etc/os-release</code></pre>

    <pre><code>uname -r</code></pre>

    <pre><code>fastfetch</code></pre>

    <p>
        Hardware information can be inspected with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lspci -k</code></pre>

    <h2>The system does not reach the desktop</h2>

    <p>
        If Octlitch stops during boot or remains on the splash screen,
        switch to a text console with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>Ctrl + Alt + F3</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check for failed system services:
    </p>

    <pre><code>systemctl --failed</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check the login manager:
    </p>

    <pre><code>systemctl status plasmalogin --no-pager -l</code></pre>

    <p>
        Inspect errors from the current boot:
    </p>

    <pre><code>journalctl -b -p err</code></pre>

    <p>
        To inspect the Plasma login manager specifically:
    </p>

    <pre><code>journalctl -b -u plasmalogin --no-pager</code></pre>

    <h3>Check Plasma package versions</h3>

    <p>
        A partially updated Plasma stack can prevent the graphical session
        from starting.
    </p>

    <pre><code>rpm -q \
kwin \
kwin-common \
kwin-libs \
kscreenlocker \
plasma-workspace \
plasma-workspace-libs \
plasma-login-manager \
libplasma</code></pre>

    <p>
        Important Plasma components should belong to the same update level.
        If they do not, synchronize the installed Fedora packages:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo dnf distro-sync --refresh</code></pre>

    <p>
        Restart afterward:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo reboot</code></pre>

    <h2>Discover is stuck on “Fetching updates”</h2>

    <p>
        Octlitch uses Flathub as its Flatpak application source.
    </p>

    <p>
        Check configured Flatpak remotes:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak remotes --show-details</code></pre>

    <p>
        A normal Octlitch installation should use Flathub. If an old Fedora
        OCI Flatpak remote is present and Discover hangs while refreshing it,
        disable that remote:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo flatpak remote-modify --system --disable fedora</code></pre>

    <p>
        If everything works correctly afterward, the unused remote can be
        removed:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo flatpak remote-delete --system fedora</code></pre>

    <p>
        Restart Discover:
    </p>

    <pre><code>killall plasma-discover 2>/dev/null || true
plasma-discover</code></pre>

    <h2>Flathub is missing</h2>

    <p>
        Check available remotes:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak remotes</code></pre>

    <p>
        Add Flathub for the current user if it is missing:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak remote-add --user --if-not-exists \
flathub \
https://flathub.org/repo/flathub.flatpakrepo</code></pre>

    <h2>Flatpak applications will not update</h2>

    <p>
        Refresh and update installed Flatpak applications:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak update</code></pre>

    <p>
        Remove unused runtimes afterward if required:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak uninstall --unused</code></pre>

    <p>
        Show detailed remote information:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak remotes --show-details</code></pre>

    <h2>DNF cannot download packages</h2>

    <p>
        First check basic network connectivity:
    </p>

    <pre><code>ping -c 3 fedoraproject.org</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check DNS resolution:
    </p>

    <pre><code>getent hosts fedoraproject.org</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check enabled repositories:
    </p>

    <pre><code>dnf repolist</code></pre>

    <p>
        Clear cached repository metadata and try again:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo dnf clean all
sudo dnf --refresh makecache</code></pre>

    <h2>Networking is not working</h2>

    <p>
        Check NetworkManager:
    </p>

    <pre><code>systemctl status NetworkManager --no-pager</code></pre>

    <p>
        Show detected network devices:
    </p>

    <pre><code>nmcli device status</code></pre>

    <p>
        Show active connections:
    </p>

    <pre><code>nmcli connection show --active</code></pre>

    <p>
        Restart NetworkManager if necessary:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager</code></pre>

    <h2>DNS is not working</h2>

    <p>
        Check whether names can be resolved:
    </p>

    <pre><code>getent hosts fedoraproject.org</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check the resolver configuration:
    </p>

    <pre><code>ls -l /etc/resolv.conf
cat /etc/resolv.conf</code></pre>

    <p>
        If the system uses systemd-resolved, inspect its state:
    </p>

    <pre><code>resolvectl status</code></pre>

    <h2>Graphics problems</h2>

    <p>
        Identify the detected graphics hardware and active kernel drivers:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lspci -k | grep -EA3 'VGA|3D|Display'</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check graphics-related kernel messages:
    </p>

    <pre><code>journalctl -b -k | grep -Ei 'drm|gpu|amdgpu|i915|xe|nvidia'</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check the current display session:
    </p>

    <pre><code>echo $XDG_SESSION_TYPE</code></pre>

    <p>
        On a normal Octlitch Plasma session this should usually report:
    </p>

    <pre><code>wayland</code></pre>

    <h2>NVIDIA driver problems</h2>

    <p>
        Verify that the NVIDIA packages are installed:
    </p>

    <pre><code>rpm -qa | grep -i nvidia</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check whether the kernel module is loaded:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lsmod | grep nvidia</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check NVIDIA status:
    </p>

    <pre><code>nvidia-smi</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check Secure Boot:
    </p>

    <pre><code>mokutil --sb-state</code></pre>

    <p>
        Rebuild the NVIDIA kernel module if necessary:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo akmods --force</code></pre>

    <p>
        See the dedicated NVIDIA chapter before making more extensive driver
        changes.
    </p>

    <h2>Audio is not working</h2>

    <p>
        Check the PipeWire user services:
    </p>

    <pre><code>systemctl --user status pipewire pipewire-pulse wireplumber</code></pre>

    <p>
        Restart the audio services:
    </p>

    <pre><code>systemctl --user restart pipewire pipewire-pulse wireplumber</code></pre>

    <p>
        Available audio devices can also be inspected with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>wpctl status</code></pre>

    <h2>Bluetooth problems</h2>

    <p>
        Check the Bluetooth service:
    </p>

    <pre><code>systemctl status bluetooth --no-pager</code></pre>

    <p>
        Restart it if necessary:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo systemctl restart bluetooth</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check detected Bluetooth devices:
    </p>

    <pre><code>bluetoothctl list</code></pre>

    <h2>A Plasma setting is behaving incorrectly</h2>

    <p>
        Log out and sign in again first. This restarts the Plasma session
        without rebooting the entire machine.
    </p>

    <p>
        For more detailed diagnostics, inspect user-session errors:
    </p>

    <pre><code>journalctl --user -b -p err</code></pre>

    <p>
        Plasma-related processes can be inspected with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>ps aux | grep -E 'plasmashell|kwin_wayland|kded6'</code></pre>

    <h2>Check disk space</h2>

    <p>
        Package installations and updates can fail when the root filesystem
        is full.
    </p>

    <pre><code>df -h</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check Flatpak storage usage:
    </p>

    <pre><code>du -sh ~/.local/share/flatpak 2>/dev/null
sudo du -sh /var/lib/flatpak 2>/dev/null</code></pre>

    <h2>View system logs</h2>

    <p>
        Errors from the current boot:
    </p>

    <pre><code>journalctl -b -p err</code></pre>

    <p>
        Kernel messages:
    </p>

    <pre><code>journalctl -b -k</code></pre>

    <p>
        Follow new system messages in real time:
    </p>

    <pre><code>journalctl -f</code></pre>

    <h2>Reporting an Octlitch problem</h2>

    <p>
        If the problem appears to be specific to Octlitch, report it through
        the project's GitHub issue tracker.
    </p>

    <p>
        Include the Octlitch release, relevant hardware information,
        commands you ran, error messages and the steps needed to reproduce
        the problem.
    </p>

    <p>
        Do not include passwords, private keys, authentication tokens or
        other secrets in logs or screenshots.
    </p>

    <p>
        <a
            href="https://github.com/OctavianIlie/octlitch/issues"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
        >
            Open the Octlitch issue tracker →
        </a>
    </p>

    <div class="docs-next">
        <a href="/documentation/">
            Back to the handbook →
        </a>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
